1
00:00:03,632 --> 00:00:05,192
The change in the architecture of the
media is

2
00:00:05,272 --> 00:00:08,053
completely connected to a change in the
architecture of control.

3
00:00:08,613 --> 00:00:09,914
With the broadcast system, you have

4
00:00:10,174 --> 00:00:13,395
one person in one station deciding what
gets put over the airwaves.

5
00:00:14,015 --> 00:00:16,036
When you have a distributed network like
the internet,

6
00:00:16,876 --> 00:00:17,856
everybody can be a server.

7
00:00:17,956 --> 00:00:20,557
There's no distinction between the
broadcaster and the receiver.

8
00:00:21,217 --> 00:00:22,979
Every computer does both. You know,

9
00:00:23,019 --> 00:00:25,322
you can take your home laptop and run a
server off of it

10
00:00:25,422 --> 00:00:27,225
that can distribute movies and music and

11
00:00:27,565 --> 00:00:29,768
web pages and email in the same way that
the

12
00:00:30,168 --> 00:00:32,391
biggest computers at Google can. There's
no

13
00:00:32,471 --> 00:00:36,015
fundamental difference between the computers they have in the
racks in their server rooms and what you have

14
00:00:36,075 --> 00:00:36,556
on your desk.

15
00:00:38,259 --> 00:00:41,524
In the old system of broadcasting, you're
fundamentally limited by the amount of

16
00:00:41,844 --> 00:00:43,146
space in the airwaves.

17
00:00:43,347 --> 00:00:46,671
You could only send out 10 channels over
the airwaves of television.

18
00:00:46,691 --> 00:00:49,596
Or even with cable, you had 500 channels.
On the internet,

19
00:00:49,636 --> 00:00:52,941
everybody can have a channel. Everyone can
get a blog or a MySpace page.

20
00:00:53,361 --> 00:00:55,102
Everyone has a way of expressing
themselves.

21
00:00:55,703 --> 00:00:57,444
And so what you see now is not a question
of

22
00:00:57,824 --> 00:00:59,785
who gets access to the airwaves. It's a
question of

23
00:01:00,125 --> 00:01:02,947
who gets control over the ways you find
people.

24
00:01:02,987 --> 00:01:05,329
You start seeing power centralizing in
sites like Google,

25
00:01:05,369 --> 00:01:08,431
these sort of gatekeepers that tell you
where on the internet you want to go,

26
00:01:08,811 --> 00:01:12,533
the people who provide you your sources of
news and information. So it's not

27
00:01:13,454 --> 00:01:16,636
only certain people have a license to speak.
Now everyone has a license to speak.

28
00:01:16,696 --> 00:01:17,877
It's a question of who gets heard.

29
00:01:19,522 --> 00:01:22,724
So one of the biggest questions we're facing
is, in a world of a million speakers,

30
00:01:22,924 --> 00:01:26,265
how do you find what's good? Are we going
to go to a system like the old media,

31
00:01:26,285 --> 00:01:30,227
where you go to CNN and they pick a handful of
people to focus on and you read what they say? Or

32
00:01:30,628 --> 00:01:32,328
are we going to go to something more like
the internet,

33
00:01:32,348 --> 00:01:36,130
where everybody has a chance at being heard,
a more democratic system? One of the

34
00:01:36,290 --> 00:01:39,972
most interesting technologies for doing something like
that is a system called collaborative filtering,

35
00:01:40,472 --> 00:01:43,673
where everybody expresses their opinions on what
they like and what they don't like,

36
00:01:44,013 --> 00:01:47,334
and the computer tries to match you up
with other people who have similar preferences

37
00:01:47,754 --> 00:01:50,695
and recommend you things that they also
like that you didn't know about before.

38
00:01:51,416 --> 00:01:52,896
It's the same kind of system you see on
Amazon,

39
00:01:52,916 --> 00:01:54,937
with people who bought this book also
bought this book.

40
00:01:55,697 --> 00:01:57,958
People are trying to experiment that not
just with books,

41
00:01:58,018 --> 00:02:01,200
but with blogs and with web pages and news
stories all across the internet

42
00:02:01,620 --> 00:02:05,602
to try and find ways of things you never would have
heard of before and bringing them in front of you.

43
00:02:06,482 --> 00:02:10,324
Mass media had this fundamental paradox
because it was aiming at a huge audience,

44
00:02:10,484 --> 00:02:12,625
but it wanted to convince everybody they
were an individual.

45
00:02:12,986 --> 00:02:16,207
So you see all these ads on television all
the time like, you know, buck the trend,

46
00:02:16,267 --> 00:02:17,468
buy these jeans, right?

47
00:02:17,748 --> 00:02:19,949
And it's on a show that four million
people are watching. You know,

48
00:02:19,969 --> 00:02:21,170
you're not going to buck a trend by

49
00:02:21,870 --> 00:02:23,651
doing the same thing four million other
people are.

50
00:02:24,331 --> 00:02:26,893
Now that the internet is actually making
these niche things possible,

51
00:02:27,454 --> 00:02:29,616
the mass media is incredibly threatened.

52
00:02:29,656 --> 00:02:32,438
No longer this idea of bucking the crowd
and being your own.

53
00:02:33,740 --> 00:02:36,162
It's no longer just a theory. You can
actually do it on the internet.

54
00:02:36,722 --> 00:02:39,024
And what we're starting to see is tools
that take power away

55
00:02:39,084 --> 00:02:41,927
from these big conglomerates and start to
distribute it to small groups.

56
00:02:41,947 --> 00:02:43,548
And so there are a bunch of issues.

57
00:02:44,469 --> 00:02:47,090
in a system like that. There are questions
of funding.

58
00:02:47,390 --> 00:02:48,691
How will these small groups get paid?

59
00:02:48,731 --> 00:02:51,312
How will the random blogger be able to
live in a way that

60
00:02:52,213 --> 00:02:55,734
investigative journalists can now because
there's one giant source of advertising?

61
00:02:56,054 --> 00:02:57,515
There are questions of finding people.

62
00:02:58,055 --> 00:02:59,916
How will I be able to find the stuff I'm
interested in,

63
00:02:59,936 --> 00:03:01,417
the stuff that's trustworthy and reliable?

64
00:03:02,397 --> 00:03:04,978
And so for each of these, there are new
technologies.

65
00:03:05,018 --> 00:03:06,638
People are trying all sorts of different
things.

66
00:03:06,678 --> 00:03:08,219
And one of the most exciting things about
the internet

67
00:03:08,579 --> 00:03:10,519
is that there's experimentation in this.

68
00:03:10,559 --> 00:03:13,060
Since everybody can just go up and start a
website with a new

69
00:03:13,460 --> 00:03:15,621
piece of technology to try and solve one
of these problems,

70
00:03:15,981 --> 00:03:18,742
we're seeing lots of different possibilities,
lots of different funding models,

71
00:03:19,102 --> 00:03:23,143
lots of different recommendation systems.
And who knows what will work best?

72
00:03:23,683 --> 00:03:25,724
We have a chance to try it all and see
what falls out.

73
00:03:26,144 --> 00:03:28,525
So there are a couple of interesting
funding models. One, of course,

74
00:03:28,585 --> 00:03:30,785
is the sort of standard method of
advertising, right?

75
00:03:31,105 --> 00:03:33,346
You go to a bunch of big corporate
sponsors,

76
00:03:33,366 --> 00:03:36,266
and instead of having them fund a television
show, you have them fund your web page.

77
00:03:37,067 --> 00:03:41,048
But a more interesting one is you do the same
thing with niche groups, right? Instead of going to

78
00:03:41,188 --> 00:03:44,989
IBM or Ford or some big company and having
them buy a banner ad on your site,

79
00:03:45,449 --> 00:03:47,729
you go to people who actually care about
the readers you have.

80
00:03:48,229 --> 00:03:50,850
If you're a design web blog, you go to
design companies, right?

81
00:03:51,349 --> 00:03:53,690
If you're a politics web blog, you go to
other politicians.

82
00:03:54,411 --> 00:03:55,671
So you have a very targeted,

83
00:03:55,711 --> 00:03:58,833
narrow group of people who are really interested
in the subject. That's a group of

84
00:03:59,273 --> 00:04:01,114
an audience that advertisers really love.

85
00:04:01,934 --> 00:04:04,555
Another possibility is to turn directly to
your readers for support.

86
00:04:04,996 --> 00:04:08,477
So you see blogs say, I want to go on a
trip to New Hampshire to cover the

87
00:04:08,717 --> 00:04:13,280
American political conventions. Will you
support me? And the readers pour in money.

88
00:04:13,920 --> 00:04:15,140
These people are very dedicated.

89
00:04:15,160 --> 00:04:17,522
They feel like they have a personal
connection with the person writing.

90
00:04:18,162 --> 00:04:20,243
So they are eager to spend money to
support it.

91
00:04:21,675 --> 00:04:23,916
Another thing is that you work simply off
of volunteer labor.

92
00:04:24,036 --> 00:04:26,977
You have people who have a day job that's
an expert in the subject,

93
00:04:27,017 --> 00:04:28,438
and they just enjoy talking about it.

94
00:04:28,898 --> 00:04:31,359
So they write stuff in their free time and
publish it on the internet.

95
00:04:31,799 --> 00:04:34,040
Or they have readers who read their site
and contribute stuff,

96
00:04:34,060 --> 00:04:36,401
and it gets compiled into one exciting
source.

97
00:04:37,422 --> 00:04:39,002
So I think there are lots of different
experiments,

98
00:04:39,262 --> 00:04:40,523
and people are trying lots of different
ways.

99
00:04:41,324 --> 00:04:44,350
I mean, that's one of the errors you
had with television, right, was that television

100
00:04:44,791 --> 00:04:49,921
could only provide one level of interest,
right? It was funded based on advertising,

101
00:04:49,941 --> 00:04:51,765
not on how much people cared about the
program.

102
00:04:53,280 --> 00:04:55,562
advertisers were going to pay the same no
matter how exciting

103
00:04:55,622 --> 00:04:58,105
or how compelling or how interested the
audience was in the show.

104
00:04:58,465 --> 00:05:01,929
So what you ended up with was fairly
boring shows that appealed to lots of people

105
00:05:02,490 --> 00:05:04,351
because that's what advertisers wanted.

106
00:05:04,371 --> 00:05:07,815
They wanted lots of people watching the
shows. Whereas in a normal market economy,

107
00:05:07,855 --> 00:05:10,218
what happens is if you really want
something, you pay more for it.

108
00:05:10,698 --> 00:05:11,919
You just can't do that with television.

109
00:05:12,800 --> 00:05:14,520
So one of the interesting things about
broadcast

110
00:05:14,600 --> 00:05:17,461
is that a lot of what you like depends on
what other people like, right?

111
00:05:17,581 --> 00:05:20,262
There are only so many shows out there.
They're all kind of bland.

112
00:05:20,282 --> 00:05:22,943
And so what happens is you have these mega
hits like American Idol

113
00:05:23,503 --> 00:05:26,924
or Lost where everybody at the water
cooler is talking about this show.

114
00:05:26,964 --> 00:05:28,924
And so you just have to watch it because
otherwise you

115
00:05:28,984 --> 00:05:33,425
can't keep up with them. Whenever social
factors get involved,

116
00:05:33,705 --> 00:05:38,086
you have this sort of process of rich gets
richer. One thing takes off because

117
00:05:38,606 --> 00:05:39,867
that's what everybody else is doing.

118
00:05:40,847 --> 00:05:42,348
One nice thing about the internet is that
it allows

119
00:05:42,428 --> 00:05:46,451
for so much more variety that niche
products can get so much more

120
00:05:47,091 --> 00:05:47,972
attention and interest.

121
00:05:48,212 --> 00:05:52,114
So they've run the numbers and there's this
proven mathematical fact that as long as

122
00:05:52,334 --> 00:05:55,356
some percentage of what you care about is
whether other people like it or not,

123
00:05:55,696 --> 00:05:58,138
you're going to end up with these patterns
of hits and failures.

124
00:05:59,643 --> 00:06:01,705
If you have two things which are
equivalent in quality,

125
00:06:02,286 --> 00:06:04,688
and one of them is liked by one more
person than the other one,

126
00:06:05,089 --> 00:06:06,530
you're going to go to that one.

127
00:06:06,570 --> 00:06:08,372
There's some small chance that you're
going to go to that one,

128
00:06:08,452 --> 00:06:11,295
and everybody's starting to start going to that one.
And all of a sudden, you have Harry Potter,

129
00:06:11,615 --> 00:06:14,978
this one book plucked out of nowhere that
suddenly becomes this humongous mega hit.

130
00:06:15,379 --> 00:06:18,102
Not because it's 100 million times better
written than every other book,

131
00:06:18,422 --> 00:06:19,923
but simply because everybody's reading it.

132
00:06:21,265 --> 00:06:23,167
And putting stuff on the internet doesn't
change that.

133
00:06:23,227 --> 00:06:24,970
You still care about what your friends
like.

134
00:06:24,990 --> 00:06:27,092
You still want to read what everybody else
is talking about.

135
00:06:27,393 --> 00:06:29,295
You still want to do what's popular
because you think

136
00:06:29,355 --> 00:06:32,159
maybe other people have a valid opinion or
maybe you want to talk to them about it.

137
00:06:32,539 --> 00:06:35,663
Maybe you want to join part of this
community. But whatever your reason is,

138
00:06:35,703 --> 00:06:37,405
as long as you care about other people's
opinions,

139
00:06:37,425 --> 00:06:38,647
you're going to end up with these hits.

140
00:06:39,127 --> 00:06:41,888
You just have this social signifier that
everybody cares about.

141
00:06:42,008 --> 00:06:45,669
Everybody's watching American Idol. It doesn't matter
how good the show is. I mean,

142
00:06:45,689 --> 00:06:47,909
it has to be somewhat decent that people
watch it.

143
00:06:48,349 --> 00:06:51,390
But once everybody's watching it and
everybody's talking about it,

144
00:06:51,430 --> 00:06:54,771
it suddenly becomes this mega hit for no
real reason, right?

145
00:06:54,811 --> 00:06:56,191
Just because it's a social phenomenon.

146
00:06:56,291 --> 00:06:59,472
And what television does is it chops off
the tail.

147
00:06:59,492 --> 00:07:03,454
It throws away all the other shows that people would
like but don't care enough about to be mega hits,

148
00:07:03,814 --> 00:07:06,615
and instead pours all of its money into
these cheap to produce shows.

149
00:07:07,415 --> 00:07:09,096
Well, you can't get rid of hits, right?

150
00:07:09,136 --> 00:07:12,197
It's a fact that people will want to do
what their friends are doing. You can't avoid that.

151
00:07:12,477 --> 00:07:15,418
But what you can do is say there's a whole
rest of the world out there.

152
00:07:15,458 --> 00:07:18,719
There's a whole rest of what people care
about other than what everybody else is doing.

153
00:07:19,199 --> 00:07:20,919
Everybody has their own particular
interests.

154
00:07:20,979 --> 00:07:22,500
Everybody has something that fascinates
them.

155
00:07:22,920 --> 00:07:25,521
And what the internet does is it allows
them to do that.

156
00:07:25,821 --> 00:07:29,083
allows them to get involved and find other
people who share these things.

157
00:07:29,704 --> 00:07:31,145
One of the exciting things about Wikipedia

158
00:07:31,585 --> 00:07:33,426
is that it doesn't just have articles on
the

159
00:07:33,747 --> 00:07:36,048
hundred most popular things or the
thousand most popular things.

160
00:07:36,368 --> 00:07:39,271
You can pick the most obscure subject on
the world and there's an article about it.

161
00:07:39,591 --> 00:07:43,013
Because for everything, there's someone
who cares a great deal about it.

162
00:07:43,514 --> 00:07:44,474
And that's what television,

163
00:07:44,494 --> 00:07:46,476
that's what radio doesn't provide, but the
internet does.

164
00:07:47,472 --> 00:07:50,253
It provides a way for you to get in touch
with those other people who really

165
00:07:50,353 --> 00:07:52,114
care about this completely obscure thing.

166
00:07:52,614 --> 00:07:56,075
So it doesn't just go in the direction of
topic. It goes in the direction of time.

167
00:07:56,115 --> 00:07:59,817
You can go back in time and find all the
shows that have been canceled, find all the articles

168
00:08:00,097 --> 00:08:00,817
that have been deleted.

169
00:08:01,277 --> 00:08:04,119
You can go back and find everything that's
been lost in major culture.

170
00:08:04,139 --> 00:08:05,539
And it's got a place on the internet,

171
00:08:05,879 --> 00:08:09,801
YouTube music videos from the 70s and 80s
that you can't find anywhere these days.

172
00:08:10,221 --> 00:08:12,022
you can watch at your leisure. Yeah, I
mean,

173
00:08:12,042 --> 00:08:14,283
I think lessening the power of the hits,
you know,

174
00:08:14,363 --> 00:08:18,145
bringing down the things at the top and making it
more egalitarian is something we should always strive for.

175
00:08:18,646 --> 00:08:21,107
It may be really difficult. It may not be
super possible.

176
00:08:21,647 --> 00:08:24,629
But it's, you know, it's something to hope
for. It's something to strive for. And

177
00:08:25,449 --> 00:08:28,811
what that means is throwing away as much
as possible

178
00:08:29,331 --> 00:08:31,512
all the things that give you hints about,
you know,

179
00:08:31,633 --> 00:08:34,194
you should do this because other people
like it. You know,

180
00:08:34,234 --> 00:08:35,655
it's very tempting when you're building

181
00:08:36,235 --> 00:08:39,957
a website or a programming system is to start
sorting things that are really popular at the top.

182
00:08:40,662 --> 00:08:44,306
But all that does is it makes it less
democratic and less fair. You have to

183
00:08:44,626 --> 00:08:48,229
have continual pressure to try and pull
things from the bottom of the tail up,

184
00:08:48,590 --> 00:08:51,893
give everybody a chance to look at
everything. And if you do that,

185
00:08:52,233 --> 00:08:53,655
maybe you won't completely get rid of
hits,

186
00:08:53,675 --> 00:08:56,517
but you can start to ameliorate some of
their problems. I mean, that's

187
00:08:56,617 --> 00:08:58,639
one power of data mining is it can start
to find

188
00:08:58,699 --> 00:09:01,822
obscure subjects that you wouldn't have
found simply because they're not popular.

189
00:09:02,403 --> 00:09:04,406
you know, one of the tools of
recommendations can be to

190
00:09:04,586 --> 00:09:06,728
pull you to the less popular stuff down in
the tail.

191
00:09:07,329 --> 00:09:10,833
The random article button on Wikipedia is really cool
in the sense, you know, you can just

192
00:09:10,894 --> 00:09:12,716
wake up every day and read about some
completely

193
00:09:12,776 --> 00:09:15,199
random topic that you'd never have heard
of except for the fact

194
00:09:15,479 --> 00:09:17,902
that there's an article in Wikipedia about
it. And boy,

195
00:09:17,922 --> 00:09:19,484
are there some completely random topics.

